# morning
## The seventeenth
You are in the haven before the day, with the stone over you, and the day goes by, as it has gone by every day of this century, as a thing that happens to other people. There are noises, now and again, through the rock. There is the drum of the Section, at eight. There is, a good deal later, a long roll, and a shout, a long way off, which is not at all like a cheer.
It is told you at dusk by Adélaïde, who comes down the stair with a lamp in her hand, and a printed sheet folded very small in the other, and stands on the third step, and reads it aloud.
It is a law. It is nine articles long. It was passed that morning, by the Convention, on the motion of a member who has been a good deal in the news. It provides that all suspected persons who are in the territory of the Republic, and who are still at large, shall be arrested at once. It says who is to be considered suspect. They are those who by their conduct, relations, remarks or writings have shown themselves partisans of tyranny. They are those who have not been able to justify their means of existence. They are those who have been refused certificates of civism. They are the former nobles, and the husbands, wives, fathers, mothers, sons, daughters, brothers, sisters and agents of emigrés. It is a very large number of persons. It is a number that nobody has been able to count.
?if b3.civism == "bought"
  She says that the house's certificate was stamped on Monday, and that it is in the drawer. She does not say what she thinks of the clerk.
?elif b3.civism == "earned"
  She says that the Section has sent round to ask when the first of the bills will be ready.
?else
  She says that the house has been marked. She says it quite steadily. She says that it is on the third list.
?end
?if b3.committee == "served"
  She says that the lists for the Committee have been set, and that they are being run off, in the cellar, on the old Swiss press. She says that it is the first time that she has heard the press go so quietly.
?elif b3.committee == "refused"
  She says that a man in a round hat has been seen twice in the street, and that he has not come in.
?end
She stops. She puts the sheet down. She says, in a rather peculiar voice, that there are going to be a great many more things said in this house, in the next year, that nobody is going to be able to take back.
-> arrest

# arrest
## The warrant
It is a little after midnight, in the second hour of the seventeenth, and you have been sent for. It is Ottaviano's card, in the old hand, left with the porter in the rue Saint-Jacques at dusk. It says only: the Hôtel. You go across the river on foot, by the Pont Neuf, with the lamps going out one by one along the quays, and into the court. It is full of torches.
There are eight of them, in the red caps and the tricolor sashes, with pikes and one lantern, and a commissary of the Section, a stout pale man in a green coat with the paper in his hand, who has been brought here from his bed and does not like it. They have a hackney coach in the court. There are two men on the steps with muskets, and two behind. They are very nervous. They have been told that this is a place of old and terrible reputation, and they have been given a warrant against a single man.
Brézac has come down to the head of the stair. He is in a clean shirt, and a plain coat of gray, with a sword at his side that he does not touch. He is smiling. He has the look of a man who has just seen the first act of a play which he understands very much better than the audience. There is a candle in his hand, which has been burning for some time, and he has not let it gutter.
He says, good evening, to the commissary. He says that he supposes that it is himself.
The commissary reads it. It is a very short warrant, and it has been written a good deal faster than it has been spelled. It says that the ci-devant Brézac, called the Chevalier de Brézac, of no profession, is to be taken to the Conciergerie, to be examined, as a suspect, and kept at the disposition of the Tribunal. He has a hand that trembles.
?if b3.sword == "warned"
  Brézac says, in a perfectly level voice, that he has been expecting it, and thanks you for having been so good.
?elif b3.sword == "hidden"
  Brézac says that he has already had the honor of a night in the cellar, and that he found it excessively restful, and that he is quite ready.
?end
?if b3.committee == "served"
  The commissary has seen you. He says that it is the citizen of the printing house, of the rue Saint-Jacques, and that it is very good, and very fortunate, that a patriot should be here. He says it with a kind of misery. He is asking you to be a witness.
?end
Brézac looks at you. He does not ask for anything. He has the courtesy, which is a courtesy of three centuries, not to make a request which it would give pain to refuse.
* Stand aside, and let them take him
  $ b3.arrest = "let"
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ bond.brezac += 1
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_law_of_suspects")
  You do not move. The soldiers go up, with their pikes, in a bunch, like men going up to a bull. He goes down to them. He gives the candle to the nearest of them, who takes it, with a kind of start, and holds it, in his big hand, like a sacred object. He says that it has been a very good house. He gets into the coach. He settles his sword on his knee, as if he were going to the opera. The commissary shuts the door. It is a quarter of an hour, in the whole, and nobody has been hurt, and it is the worst quarter of an hour of the year.
* Take the commissary aside, and buy the month
  $ b3.arrest = "paper"
  $ do earn(-7)
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ standing += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ bond.brezac += 2
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_law_of_suspects")
  It takes three minutes, in a corner of the court, under the arch, and a good deal of paper. You say that the examination is a very great matter, and that it would be a pity if the citizen were to be questioned in a hurry, and that there are persons who would take a great interest in the order in which names are put. The commissary does not look at you. He nods. He takes the purse, with a kind of horror. He says that it is not in his power to prevent it, but that it is in his power to be late. Brézac is taken, all the same, and goes down the steps with his candle. At the door of the coach, he turns, and, with a courteous inclination, sets his hat very slightly on one side. It is a thing that men do who have just been dealt a good hand.
* Go with the coach, as far as the gate of the Conciergerie
  $ b3.arrest = "followed"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ bond.brezac += 3
  $ do chart("paris.court")
  $ do discover("the_law_of_suspects")
  You walk behind it, through the dark, with your hands in your pockets, as a man might walk behind a hearse. It goes by the Pont Neuf, and along the quay, and in by the great gate of the Palace, where the torch bearers are lit. You stand in the shadow. There is an official at the grille with a lantern, who reads the warrant, with great ceremony, and writes a number. Brézac gets down. He says, as he goes through, without turning his head, in the voice that he has always used for the end of a duel, that it would be a very great kindness to say nothing to the Prince. It is the last thing that he says. The wicket closes.
$ do rest()
